  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts a standard TEMPO-based aqueous electrolyte with an ionic-liquid-style TEMPO dimer designed to behave more like a “water-in-salt” system. On the left, conventional TEMPO is shown as a single radical molecule dissolved in water, with many free water molecules surrounding relatively few solute particles. This suggests a typical dilute “TEMPO-in-water” environment, where water remains largely unbound and the achievable capacity is low, indicated by the small battery icon of about 10 Ah L⁻¹.\n\nOn the right, the TEMPO units are chemically linked into a charged dimer with a counterion, creating a much more concentrated, ionic-liquid-like electrolyte. The accompanying cartoon shows water molecules interacting closely with ions rather than existing mostly as free water. Visually, the balance shifts from water-rich to active-material-rich, with larger yellow solute spheres and fewer freely dispersed water molecules. This composition is presented as analogous to water-in-salt behavior, but based on TEMPO species.\n\nThe overall message is that converting TEMPO into an ionic, dimeric form increases the concentration of redox-active material, changes how water is coordinated, and greatly boosts volumetric energy-storage capability, reaching roughly 100 Ah L⁻¹.",
